// #4084: on the gc lane a struct literal bound to a local is declared as
// `(ref null $t)` in the locals header, and that type index is a HEAP TYPE --
// a signed LEB128 (s33). It was written unsigned, which differs exactly when
// the last 7-bit group has bit 6 set. Struct types start at 14, so `S50` is
// type 64 (`0x40`, read as -64: "unknown heap type") and `S96` is type 110
// (`0x6E`, read as the abstract `any`, so the local became `anyref` and
// `struct.get` failed validation). The build reported success either way.
//
// 120 declarations put both bands in range; `S10` (type 24) is the control
// that worked before. Each literal sits in a top-level function because a
// lambda -- which is what a `test` body is lowered to -- does not use the
// native struct lane, so a literal written in the test itself would not reach
// the locals header at all. Run on the gc lane by the `gc-gate (validate)` job.

struct S0 {
  a: Int;
  b: Int
}

struct S1 {
  a: Int;
  b: Int
}

struct S2 {
  a: Int;
  b: Int
}

struct S3 {
  a: Int;
  b: Int
}

struct S4 {
  a: Int;
  b: Int
}

